itute of human rights  and i also  i'm a member of the house of lords i'm a  parliamentarian i'm in the legislature  and we've recently been calling upon the  british government  uh to create um uh uh to make a decision  about uh whether  you should trade with a country that  might be committing genocide  and and the evidence has been coming  through  very clearly particularly over the last  few years  that what is happening to the uyghur has  all the pointers  of of of genocide taking place  that people are having their identity  removed their their sense of themselves  as muslims  as as people who speak uh their own  language  people who have their own culture that  that's not to be allowable  inside contemporary china  and so they are experiencing terrible  abuses of human rights  on an industrial scale to use the words  of our  foreign secretary and so we were urging  the the government to create  sanctions against leading chinese  officials and we were calling upon our  government  to have in mind when they are making  trade negotiations  um that uh it would be absolutely  abhorrent  to be trading with a nation that was  conducting a genocide  well let's talk about that i want to  play uh you something that british prime  minister boris johnson said last week  before the announcement  of today's sanctions about china take a  listen  there is no question that china will  pose a  great challenge for an open society such  as ours but we will also  work with china where that is consistent  with our values and interests including  building a stronger and positive  economic relationship  and in addressing climate change  now miss kennedy prime minister boris  johnson there is straddling two things  and  in all fairness he's not the only  western leader to do this decoupling  human rights  issues from economic interest is it  possible to compartmentalize  values and trade basically have you know  the cake and eat it as well  i think i think that um every government  i